# XSH pclose()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Streams come from popen() (xsh/popen). Every wait is guarded by an alarm
# that fails the test, so a pclose() that never returns is a failure, not a
# hang.

assertion  XSH.pclose.001
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pclose.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() returns the termination status of the shell in wait
           format: for "exit 7" WIFEXITED is true and WEXITSTATUS is 7.
test       t/001-status.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.002
anchor     functions/pclose.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       For a shell killed by a signal ("kill -s KILL $$") pclose()
           returns a status with WIFSIGNALED true and WTERMSIG SIGKILL.
test       t/002-signalled.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.003
anchor     functions/pclose.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     utilities/V3_chap02.html#2.9.1.4
class      required
since      1988
says       For a command naming a utility that does not exist the status
           is WIFEXITED with WEXITSTATUS 127.
test       t/003-not-found.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.004
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() waits for the command to end: after a command that
           sleeps 1 s and then writes a file, the file exists when
           pclose() returns, and the call took about 1 s.
test       t/004-waits.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.005
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() closes the stream: the descriptor that was under it is
           no longer open afterwards (fcntl(fd, F_GETFD) fails with EBADF).
test       t/005-closes.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.006
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       Data written to a "w" stream and not flushed reaches the command
           by the time pclose() has returned ("cat > out").
note       Closing a stream flushes it (XSH 2.5 / fclose()).
test       t/006-flushes.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.007
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() does not affect the status of other children: one that
           exited before the call and one that exits during it can both be
           waited for afterwards with their own exit values.
test       t/007-other-children.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.008
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pclose.html#ERRORS/ECHILD
class      required
since      1988
says       When the caller itself has reaped the command's shell with
           waitpid(-1, ...), pclose() returns -1 with errno ECHILD.
test       t/008-echild.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.009
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pclose.html#RATIONALE
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() does not give up when a signal is caught while it waits
           (SIGALRM without SA_RESTART, after 1 s): it returns the shell's
           status after a 2 s command has ended, not -1.
test       t/009-signal.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.010
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pclose.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With two popen() streams open, pclose() of each returns the status
           of its own command, whichever is closed first (exit 3 and exit
           4, closed in the reverse of the opening order).
test       t/010-two-streams.c

assertion  XSH.pclose.011
anchor     functions/pclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       pclose() of a "r" stream whose command would write forever
           returns: the stream is closed so that the command stops (is
           killed by SIGPIPE, or fails), and the status then is that of a
           shell that has ended.
note       The caller restores the default action of SIGPIPE before
           popen(), so the command inherits it. Only the return is
           required: either end of the command is allowed.
test       t/011-closes-first.c
